                                            "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron\u0027s exceptional long-term investment returns, attributing the outcome primarily to conviction-based, concentrated positions held for extended periods Ã¢â‚¬â€ most notably a split-adjusted Tesla entry at $14 per share in 2014 and a roughly $25 billion stake in SpaceX built since 2017. Baron Capital grew from roughly $10 million to $70 billion in AUM, with Baron Partners Fund significantly outperforming SPY, QQQ, and Russell Midcap Growth indices over comparable periods. Brown\u0027s argument is that the same investments were available to anyone, but committee structures, fund charters, and position-size limits prevented most institutional managers from replicating the outcome.",
                                            "take":  "The Baron case study is less about stock picking genius and more about institutional structure as the binding constraint on returns Ã¢â‚¬â€ a point that has direct relevance for how enterprise AI investment decisions get made inside large organizations. The managers who will compound best on AI-era opportunities are the ones with the authority to hold concentrated positions through volatility, not the ones constrained by quarterly benchmark hugging. For individual investors, the Baron Tesla/SpaceX story is an uncomfortable reminder that conviction without position-size freedom is worthless: identifying the right company 10 years early but capping exposure at 2% of the portfolio produces mediocre outcomes. The structural lesson is more useful than the stock tip.",
                                            "isNew":  true
