                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran revisits the long-running debate over how rising U.S. Treasury rates in 2026 affect equity valuations across sectors. He finds that while stocks have generally held up despite rate increases, they underperform materially on days of significant yield spikes. Rising corporate earnings have so far cushioned the blow, but Damodaran argues the key driver of rates is fundamentalsÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âinflation expectations and economic growthÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Ânot Fed policy alone, making the equity-rate relationship more nuanced than the simple inverse often cited.",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran is doing investors a service by cutting through the noise: blaming the Fed for high rates is a lazy shortcut. If rates are elevated because earnings and growth are strong, equities can absolutely coexist with a 5%-yield environment. The real risk is a scenario where rates rise on inflation fears while earnings disappointÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âthat double squeeze is what investors should model for, not the rate level in isolation.",
