                                                                                "title":  "The Scaling and Profitability Trade off: Venture Capital\u0027s Weakest Link!",
                                                                                "source":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                                                "author":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                                                "url":  "https://aswathdamodaran.blogspot.com/2026/09/the-scaling-and-profitability-trade-off.html",
                                                                                "date":  "2026-09-02",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgh5fXUk3rKQgi_I28N1acK-5yXvwUPErlVboSe6pyCHZL8x0-r5qMMHlHdVhSxhBxFeuLGARgSFuwvCTDO_AdIgPsSjnpLeGDHHWmsXuT9hy7GID8Rkfe5QL_54HQTfOG1JUheL-vNqI2rSUlHl79wOqBiuwCGhvwRaQkpP2pQrPQ_PantALROtViRqHw/s320/Khoslatweet.jpg",
                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran takes aim at the VC industry\u0027s structural bias toward scale over profitability, arguing that not every business is suited for hyper-growth and that VC incentive structures reward exits at high valuation multiples regardless of underlying economics. He documents a stark historical shift: private companies now go public at record valuations while still burning cash, a pattern that destroys long-term value for public-market investors. The post contends that founders are being pushed toward growth strategies that benefit VCs at IPO but leave public shareholders holding the bag.",
                                                                                "take":  "This is a useful framework for evaluating any AI or tech IPO pipeline. When a company\u0027s VC backers have already captured the value creation through valuation-multiple exits, the public market offering is often a liquidity event for insiders, not a growth opportunity for buyers. Investors should demand a credible, near-term path to profitability before buying into any VC-backed IPOÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âthe era of rewarding scale for its own sake is over, or should be.",
                                                                                "isNew":  true
