                                                                                "source":  "Downtown Josh Brown",
                                                                                "author":  "Josh Brown",
                                                                                "url":  "https://www.downtownjoshbrown.com/p/we-ain-t-seen-nothing-yet",
                                                                                "date":  "2026-08-02",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/fit=scale-down,quality=80,format=auto,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/edc330cd-79d0-4826-a3a5-bf12f41ad6a3/alex_kantrowitz.png?t=1785677394",
                                                                                "summary":  "Josh Brown interviews Big Technology journalist Alex Kantrowitz, who argues that Wall Street is badly underestimating future compute demand. Rather than worrying about current capex excess, tech leaders are laser-focused on the agentic AI eraÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âwhere billions of people run persistent AI assistants completing tasks around the clockÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âwhich will require orders of magnitude more infrastructure than exists today. Kantrowitz contends that today\u0027s hyperscaler buildout is not overinvestment; it is preparation for a demand wave that hasn\u0027t arrived yet.",
                                                                                "take":  "This is a direct rebuttal to the bear case on AI infrastructure spending. If the agentic transition plays outÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âand the trajectory of model capability improvements suggests it willÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âthen current compute investment looks conservative in hindsight. Investors who sold Nvidia and data-center REITs on \u0027capex fatigue\u0027 narratives may be making the same mistake as those who called broadband overbuilding in 1999 without accounting for what streaming would eventually demand. The infrastructure bull case is not dead; it is early.",
                                                                                "isNew":  true
