                                                                                "title":  "How Ron Baron returned over $60 billion to his investors: Secrets from the top performing mutual fund in America since 2003",
                                                                                "source":  "Downtown Josh Brown",
                                                                                "author":  "Josh Brown",
                                                                                "url":  "https://www.downtownjoshbrown.com/p/how-ron-baron-returned-over-60-billion-to-his-investors",
                                                                                "date":  "2026-07-25",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/fit=scale-down,quality=80,format=auto,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/eaff9351-21ae-4e4c-88d6-883dcd1d79bb/ron_baron.png?t=1784993976",
                                                                                "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron of Baron Capital, whose fund has been the top-performing U.S. mutual fund since 2003, growing from $10 million to roughly $70 billion in AUM. Baron\u0027s edge comes from extreme conviction and unusually long holding periodsÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âhis early Tesla position and a large SpaceX stake are emblematic of his approach: back visionary founders and hold through volatility. The post highlights how Baron\u0027s philosophy of ignoring short-term noise and compounding through multi-decade positions is what separates him from virtually every peer manager.",
                                                                                "take":  "Baron\u0027s record is a powerful data point against the cult of active trading and factor rotation. His outperformance is not from a smarter processÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âit\u0027s from having the psychological infrastructure to hold a winner for a decade when everyone else rotates out. For most investors, the actionable lesson is not \u0027find the next Tesla early\u0027 but rather \u0027stop selling your winners to harvest gains.\u0027 Turnover is the silent killer of long-term compounding, and Baron\u0027s career is the clearest proof.",
